Adsorption of Fluoride to UiO-66-NH2 in Water:
Stability, Kinetic, Isotherm and Thermodynamic
## Studies
Abstract
To provide safe drinking water, fluoride in water must be removed and adsorption processes appear to be the most widely used method. Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) represent a new class of adsorbents that have been used in various adsorption applications. To study the adsorption mechanism of fluoride to MOFs in water and obtain related adsorption parameters, we synthesized a zirconium-based MOF with a primary amine group on its ligand, named UiO-66-NH2. The kinetics, adsorption isotherm and thermodynamics of fluoride adsorption to UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were investigated. The crystalline structure of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ remained intact and the local structure of zirconium in UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ did not change significantly after being exposed to fluoride. The kinetics of the fluoride adsorption in UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ could be well represented by the pseudo second order rate law. The enthalpy of the adsorption indicates that the $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ adsorption to UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ was classified as a physical adsorption. However, the comparison between the adsorption capacities of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and UiO-66 suggests that the fluoride adsorption to UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ might primarily involve a strong interaction between $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ and the metal site. The fluoride adsorption capacity of UiO-66-NH2 was found to decrease when $\mathrm{pH}~>~7$ . While the presence of chloride/bromide ions did not noticeably change the adsorption capacity of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ , the ionic surfactants slightly affected the adsorption capacity of UiO-66-NH2. These findings provide insights to further optimize the adsorption process for removal of fluoride using zirconium-based MOFs.
Keywords: Metal organic frameworks, Fluoride, UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , X-ray adsorption
## 1. Introduction
Fluoride can be found in natural water systems resulted from the dissolution of fluoride-containing minerals [1, 2] and wastewater discharged from manufacturing processes using fluorine compounds [2]. In order to provide safe drinking water as proposed by World Health Organization (WHO), the concentration of fluoride in water must be controlled to meet the permissible limits of fluoride, $1.5\mathrm{\mg\L^{-1}}$ . Otherwise, it can cause serious human health issues, such as molting of teeth, and lesion of endocrine glands, thyroid, and liver as well as other organs [3, 4].
Typically, removal of fluoride, also called defluoridation, from water can be achieved using precipitation [5, 6], ion-exchange processes [7, 8], dialysis [4, 9, 10] and adsorption processes [2, 11-14]. Although each technique has its own merits, the adsorption is still one of the most employed methods owing to its low initial cost and straightforward operation [15]. Therefore, developing high-capacity and selective adsorbents is essential to facilitate fluoride removal in order to meet the requirement for the drinking water.
Recently, a new class of adsorbents, named Metal Organic Framworks (MOFs), attracts great attention because of their tunable crystalline organic-inorganic hybrid networks [16-18]. MOFs can exhibit superior physical and chemical characteristics and therefore they have been applied in various adsorption-related areas, including hydrogen storage [19, 20], $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption [21, 22] and removal of toxic gases [23, 24]. Owing to MOFs’ promising potential in the areas mentioned above, researchers start examining MOFs’ capability to adsorb compounds in aqueous environments. A number of studies have been conducted including removal of metal ions [25], toxic dyes [26, 27], herbicides [28], oil droplets [29], humic acid [30]. These studies unveil that several types of MOFs can remain intact in aqueous solutions and show exceptional capacities to remove pollutants from water compared to traditional adsorbents, like granular activated carbon [27, 29].
Lately, MOFs are also evaluated for their defluoridation capacity in water [31, 32]. It has been found that a zirconium-containing MOF, UiO-66, exhibited a relatively high adsorption capacity for fluoride compared to other MOFs consisting of iron, aluminum, chromium, zinc and hafnium. While UiO-66 has been demonstrated for its high defluoridation capacity, the stability, kinetics, equilibrium isotherm, and thermodynamics of using UiO-66 to adsorb fluoride in water have not been further investigated thoroughly. Additionally, the ligand in the zirconium-based MOF can be inserted with amine functional groups which are expected to facilitate the adsorption of fluoride via the electrostatic attraction [33]. Therefore, we prepared a UiO-66 with a primary amine group on its ligand to form UiO-66-NH2. The as-prepared UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was characterized using Scanning Electronic Microscopy (SEM), Transmission Electronic Microscopy (TEM), X-ray Diffraction (XRD), FT-IR, thermogravimetric analyzer (TGA) and X-ray adsorption spectroscopy (XAS). To examine the stability and the structure change before and after the adsorption of fluoride, XRD was used to observe any changes on the crystalline structure of UiO-66-NH2. In terms of changes in local structure of $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , spectra of Zr K-edge X-Ray Adsorption Near Edge Structure (XANES) spectroscopy and Extended X-Ray Adsorption Fine Structure (EXAFS) spectroscopy of UiO-66-NH2 were acquired after the exposure to fluoride under various conditions. Adsorption kinetics and adsorption isotherm were determined and analyzed. Thermodynamic parameters of the fluoride adsorption to UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ were also obtained. To evaluate the capacity of UiO-66-NH2 under various conditions, effects of pH of solution, co-existing halogen ions and ionic surfactants on the adsorption capacity were also investigated.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1 Preparation of UiO-66-NH2
UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ was prepared according to the reported studies [34, 35], and detailed synthesis and characterization procedures can be found in the electronic supporting information (Text S1, see ESI†).
## 2.2 Fluoride adsorption to UiO-66-NH2
To determine the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ for fluoride ion, batch-type experiments were employed. Typically, 0.01 g of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ was mixed with $20~\mathrm{ml}$ of fluoride solution with an initial concentration $(C_{0})$ of $20~\mathrm{mg~L}^{-1}$ in a $25\mathrm{-ml}$ glass vial. The glass vial then was immediately placed in a temperature-controllable orbital shaker at $300\mathrm{rpm}$ for a certain mixing time, $t$ , at a pre-set temperature. The adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ at the mixing time $t$ was quantified using the following equation (Eq.(1)):
where $C_{t}$ $(\log\mathbf{L}^{-1})$ denotes a remaining concentration of fluoride in a solution, m (g) represents the weight of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ added to the solution, and $V$ is the total volume (L) of the solution. The adsorption capacity at equilibrium was denoted as $q_{e}$ . Experimental procedures for investigating effects of solid-to-liquid ratio, temperature, pH, co-existing ions and surfactants can be found in the electronic supporting information (Text S2 and S3, see ESI†).
## 3. Results and Discussion
3.1 Characterization of the as-synthesized UiO-66-NH2
The morphology of the as-synthesized UiO-66- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ can be seen in Fig. 1(a), in which UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ exhibits an octahedron-like appearance. The average edge length of these UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ crystals was $100(\pm10)\$ nm, revealing their nanoscale sizes. UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ nanocrystals can be separated from each other and form a single octahedron as shown in Fig. 1(b). To further characterize the chemical structure of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , an infrared spectrum of UiO-66-NH2 was measured and shown in Fig. 2(a). Since the ligand in UiO-66-NH2 was 2-aminoterephthlic acid, an amine-functionalized terephthalic acid, which mainly consists of aromatic carboxylates. Therefore, there are several peaks derived from aromatic and carboxylic groups in the IR spectrum: $1420~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ from C-C vibrational mode, and $1580~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ from C-O bonding in the carboxylates [35]. Considering that the ligand also contains a primary amine group bound to the aromatic ring, two characteristic peaks of the primary amine can be detected at 3515 and $3390~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , attributing to asymmetric and symmetric stretching modes of N-H bonding, respectively [35, 36]. The bonding between aromatic carbon and nitrogen, $\mathrm{\bfC}_{\mathrm{ar}^{-}}\mathrm{\bfN}$ , could be also observed at 1260 and $1370~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ [35]. Fig. 2(b) also reveals the XRD pattern of the as-synthesized UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ which is identical to the simulated and the reported XRD patterns of UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ [37, 38].
Fig. S1 (see ESI†) depicts the thermogravimetric (TG) curve of the as-synthesized UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , which agrees with the reported TG curve of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ [36, 39]. A series of significant weight losses occurred during the thermal decomposition starting from $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ all the way to $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . This series of weight losses could be attributed to the decomposition of the ligand, 2-aminoterephthalic acid [39]. The residue remained in the end of the decomposition was primarily zirconium oxides that were derived from the “node” of UiO-66- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption and desorption isotherms of $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ can be seen in Fig. S2 and the isotherm curves reveal that $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ comprises micropores as typically seen in UiO-66-based MOFs [40, 41]. The BET surface area and total pore volume were found to be $905~\mathrm{{m}^{2}~\mathrm{{g}^{-1}}}$ and 0.43 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which are also similar to the reported values [42].
3.2  Effects of water and fluoride on stability and chemical structure of UiO-66-NH2 Since UiO-66-NH2 is used to remove fluoride from water, the stability of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ exposed to water and fluoride must be examined to ensure its structure during the adsorption process. To this end, we measured the XRD pattern of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ after being exposed to water and fluoride-containing water ( $\scriptstyle{C o=50}{\mathrm{~mg}}$ ${\bf L}^{-1}$ ). Fig. 2(b) shows the XRD patterns of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ under various conditions. It can be seen that within $2\uptheta=10^{-}60^{\circ}$ , the XRD peaks of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ remain the same after being exposed to water and the fluoride-containing water. This indicates that the crystalline structure of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ was highly stable in water and remained intact in contact with fluoride, suggesting the promising potential of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in aqueous applications.
Since UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ consists of zirconium $(\mathrm{Zr})$ as the node in the framework, it is critical to determine whether water and fluoride could affect the local geometric and electronic structure of zirconium in $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . Thus, we used X-ray adsorption spectroscopy (XAS) to investigate the local order around cationic zirconium in contact with fluoride under various conditions. Fig. 3(a) reveals the Zr $K$ -edge
XANES spectra of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ samples after being exposed to fluoride. It can be seen that the spectrum of pristine UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ showed an absorption edge at 18013 eV and a white line at 18021 eV followed by a hump at 18040 eV. Such spectral features are quite similar to those of $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ [43]. Even though zirconium might not react with oxygen to form crystalline $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ , the spectral features suggest that a strong interaction could exist between zirconium and oxygen in 2-aminoterephthalic acid. After UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ being exposed to other anions and acid/base, the spectral features remained quite comparable to those of the pristine UiO-66-NH2, suggesting that the interaction between zirconium and oxygen in the ligand was not significantly interfered.
Fig. 3 (b) displays EXAFS signals of UiO-66-NH2 samples under the various conditions. Patterns of EXAFS signals of these treated samples are all similar to that of pristine UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , showing that the bonding of Zr-O was highly stable in UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ even after reacting with fluoride under the various conditions. The Fourier Transformed EXAFS data (Fig. 3(c)) also reveal the similar feature as seen in the XANES analysis, confirming that the bonding of $Z\mathrm{r-O}$ was not significantly changed after being exposed to fluoride. Thus, UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ exhibited a high stability after being exposed to fluoride, water and other ions.
## 3.3 Effect of solid-to-liquid ratio on adsorption capacity of UiO-66-NH2
Prior to investigating kinetics of fluoride adsorption on $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , it is critical to examine the effect of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ loading on its fluoride removal capability. Fig. S3 shows the removal efficiency (i.e., $(C_{0}–C_{t})/C_{0}\%)$ as a function of the solid-to-liquid (S/L) ratio. The removal efficiency gradually increased from 19 to $51\%$ as the S/L ratio increased from 0.125 to 0.75. The higher removal efficiency at the higher S/L ratio was attributed to more reaction sites of UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ available present in the solution. However, when the $\mathrm{{S/L}}$ ratio continued to increase, the removal efficiency of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ became stable and approached a constant value starting from the S/L ratio $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}0.5$ . The effect of S/L ratio can be associated with the surface site heterogeneity model [44, 45]. When the surface sites of an adsorbent are heterogeneous, its surface contains adsorption sites with a spectrum of binding energies. At a low $\mathrm{{S/L}}$ ratio, all types of sites are fully exposed and therefore the F adsorption to $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was saturated faster. On the contrary, when S/L ratio is increased, the availability of high binding energies decreases and most of lower energy sites have been occupied. result, the adsorption capacity (mg-adsorbate/g-adsorbent) became smaller and the removal efficiency approached to a constant [44]. Thus, this ratio of 0.5 was adopted for the rest of experiments in this study.
3.4 Effects of mixing time and temperature on adsorption capacity of UiO-66-NH2 Fig. 4(a) shows the adsorption kinetics at various temperatures. At $20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , more than $90\%$ of the equilibrium adsorption capacity, $q_{e}$ , could be achieved in $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ and the adsorption equilibrium was reached within $30~\mathrm{min}$ . Once temperature went up to 40 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the $q_{e}$ of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ considerably decreased, revealing that an increase in the temperature of solution caused a negative effect on the adsorption capacity. As the temperature of solution continued to rise to $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the $q_{e}$ was further decreased, suggesting that the fluoride adsorption to $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ could be an exothermic reaction.
To analyze the kinetic data, we used two models: the pseudo first order and the pseudo second rate laws. The pseudo first order rate law can be expressed as follows (Eq.(2)):
$$
\ln(q_{e}-q_{t})=\ln q_{e}-{\frac{k_{1}}{2.303}}t
$$
where $k_{1}(\mathrm{min}^{-1})$ is the pseudo first order rate constant. The fitting results can be seen as the dotted lines in Fig. 4(a). One can see that the kinetic data could  not be well-fitted using the pseudo first order rate law with relatively low correlation coefficients $({R_{1}}^{2})$ (Table 1). We further modeled the kinetic data using the pseudo second order rate law which can be described in the following equation (Eq.(3)):
$$
\frac{t}{q_{t}}=\frac{1}{k_{2}{q_{e}}^{2}}+\frac{t}{q_{e}}
$$
where $k_{2}$ $(\mathrm{g\mg^{-1}\ m i n^{-1}})$ represents the pseudo second order rate constant. The fitting results are represented by the solid lines in Fig. 4(a). Correlation coefficients $({R_{2}}^{2})$ of the fitting results can be also found in Table 1 which shows that all ${R_{2}}^{2}$ at different temperatures all exceed 0.980, revealing that the pseudo second rate law is more satisfactory to represent the kinetic data. Table 1 also lists the fitted equilibrium adsorption capacity $(q_{\mathrm{e,fitted}})$ , the kinetic constant $(k_{2})$ , as well as the initial sorption rate h, at $\mathrm{\Deltat{\rightarrow}0}$ which is calculated using the following equation (Eq.(4)):
$$
h=k_{2}{q_{e}}^{2}
$$
In comparison with the experimental $q_{e}(i.e.,q_{\mathrm{e,exp}})$ , the $q_{\mathrm{e,fitted}}$ obtained by the pseudo second order rate law is closer than the pseudo first order rate law, validating the satisfactory applicability of the pseudo second order rate law.
On the other hand, the kinetic constant, $k_{2}$ , is found to increase as the temperature rose up. This indicates that even though the elevated temperature led to a low $q_{e}$ , the elevated temperature could still facilitate the adsorption kinetics.
Apart from the rate laws, the intraparticle diffusion model was used to analyze the kinetic data to provide insights to the diffusion mechanism using the following equation (Eq.(5)):
$$
q_{t}=k_{P,i}t^{\frac{1}{2}}+C
$$
where $k_{P,i}$ means the diffusion rate constant at phase $i$ and $C$ represents the intercept, associated with thickness of the boundary layer and also related to the external mass transfer during the adsorption [46]. An adsorption process generally involves three phases. In the first phase, adsorbate diffuses from the bulk solution to external surface of adsorbent, so-called “boundary layer diffusion” [47]. During the second phase, remaining concentration of the adsorbate significantly decreases and therefore the intraparticle diffusion becomes slow [48]. Subsequently, the diffusion moves toward the third phase: the equilibrium state of the adsorption process [49, 50].
Based on the Eq. (5), plots of $q_{t}$ versus $t^{0.5}$ at different temperatures are displayed in Fig. S4. To focus on the diffusion behavior, only the first and second phases of the diffusion are shown in Fig. S4. Each plot of $q_{t}$ versus $t^{0.5}$ can be distinguished by two linearities, representing the first and second phases of the diffusion. The diffusion rates, $k_{p,i},$ (i.e., slopes of the linearities) of fluoride to UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ are summarized in Table S1. Correlation coefficients of the first and second linearities are higher than 0.95, showing that the data points could be well fitted by the linear regression. The boundary layer diffusion rates, $k_{p,I}$ , are found to be much higher than the intraparticle diffusion, $k_{p,2}$ , at the testing temperatures, showing that the boundary layer diffusion was significantly faster the intraparticle diffusion.
## 3.5 Adsorption isotherm of fluoride adsorption using UiO-66-NH2
Fig. 4(b) shows plots of $C_{e}$ versus $q_{e}$ of the fluoride adsorption at different temperatures. It can be seen that the experimental equilibrium adsorption capacity, $q_{e,e x p}$ , reaches around $55~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ at $20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . However, $q_{e,e x p}$ became $45~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ when the temperature changed to $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $34~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . This trend agrees with the result of kinetic data at different temperatures, showing the negative effect on the adsorption isotherm at higher temperatures. To quantitatively analyze the adsorption isotherm data, we employed four adsorption isotherm models, including Langmuir, Freundlich, Temkin as well as Dubinin-Radushkevich (D-R) isotherms.
In the Langmuir isotherm model, the adsorption process is assumed to occur as a mono-layer on a uniform surface where the adsorption sites are limited. Once the adsorption sites are occupied by adsorbate, these sites cannot be available for the adsorption anymore. Therefore, a maximal adsorption capacity can be predicted using the Langmuir isotherm model. The Langmuir isotherm model can be expressed as follows:
$$
\frac{C_{e}}{q_{e}}=\frac{1}{K_{L}q_{\mathrm{max}}}+\frac{C_{e}}{q_{\mathrm{max}}}^{\mathrm{~\tiny~\4~}}
$$
where $K_{L}$ is the Langmuir adsorption constant and $q_{m a x}$ is the maximal adsorption capacity. Fig. S5(a) displays plots of $C_{e}$ versus $C_{e}/q_{e}$ at different temperatures, in that the data points are all well fitted by the linear regression, suggesting that the adsorption isotherm data can be described by the Langmuir isotherm. According to the Eq.(6), $q_{m a x}$ and $K_{L}$ are also determined and summarized in Table 2. The predicted $q_{m a x}$ of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is found to be $58.82~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ at $20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and becomes 52.63 and $41.66\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}$ at 40 and $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively. In a previous study, Zhao et al. found that at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , UiO-66 could exhibit an equilibrium adsorption capacity of $44.92~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ . Since the tested temperature in this study was not exactly $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , we estimated the equilibrium fluoride adsorption capacity of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ by the interpolation and it is $55.70~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ . This value suggests that the fluoride adsorption capacity of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was slight higher than UiO-66, possibly owing to the amine group on the ligand in UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ [33]. In the present study, the adsorption isotherm for fluoride adsorption using UiO-66 is also shown in Fig. 4(b). The corresponding maximal adsorption capacity and the reported value from Zhao et al. at other temperature are also summarized in Table S2 (see ESI†). It can be seen that UiO-66 exhibited slightly a lower (i.e., ${\sim}20\%)$ ) adsorption capacity than UiO-66-NH2. Although the difference seemed to be attributed to the presence of amine group, the main adsorption capacity might be owing to the original structure of UiO-66, which accounted for ${\sim}80\%$ of the total adsorption capacity. It has been reported that the metal sites in MOFs can be partially positively-charged [51-53] because of unsaturated coordination on metal sites. The metal site (i.e., Zr in UiO-66 and UiO-66- $\mathrm{.NH}_{2}$ ) can interact with negatively-charged sites through the electrostatic interaction. Thus, despite that the amine group could improve the $\mathrm{F^{-}}$ adsorption, the main mechanism might be owing to the metal sites in UiO-66 and UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ . In addition, a table has been prepared in (Table S2, see ESI†) to list a number of the reported adsorbents for the $\mathrm{F}$ adsorption. According to a review article edited by Bhatnagar et al.[14], typical adsorbents for fluoride include minerals, chitosan, alumina, metal oxides, etc. Nevertheless, most of these adsorbents exhibit quite limited adsorption capacities $(i.e.,<30\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . Only a few adsorbents (e.g., facilely-prepared metal oxides) can show relatively high adsorption capacities as Table S2 shows.
On the other hand, the semi-empirical isotherm model, the Freundlich isotherm, was also used to analyze the adsorption isotherm data. The Freundlich isotherm assumes that both mono-layer and multiple-layer adsorptions can occur during an adsorption process. Thus, the Freundlich isotherm takes the chemisorption and physisorption into account. The Freundlich isotherm can be expressed in the following equation (Eq.(7)):
$$
\ln q_{e}=\ln K_{{\scriptscriptstyle F}}+\frac{1}{n}\ln C_{e}
$$
where $K_{F}$ represents the Freundlich constant and $1/\mathrm{n}$ is the heterogeneity factor of the adsorbent. Fig. S5(b) shows the fitting of the isotherm data points using the Freundlich isotherm model. The correlation coefficients (Table 2) reveal that the Freundlich model is less satisfactory to describe the isotherm data at low temperatures.
Next, the Temkin isotherm model was adopted to analyze the adsorption isotherm data. In the Temkin isotherm model, the adsorption energy is assumed to decrease proportionally with the surface coverage owing to the adsorbate-adsorbent interaction. Therefore, the Temkin isotherm can be described as follows (Eq.(8)):
$$
q_{e}=\frac{R T}{b}\ln K_{\scriptscriptstyle T}+\frac{R T}{b}\ln C_{e}
$$
where $b$ denotes the Temkin model constant and $K_{T}$ is the Temkin isotherm constant. Fig. S5(c) shows the fitting of the adsorption data points using the Temkin isotherm. Correlation coefficients (Table 2) derived from the Temkin isotherm are all higher than 0.98 at the testing temperatures, showing that the adsorption isotherm data can be also well-described by the Temkin isotherm model.
Moreover, we chose the D-R isotherm model to investigate whether the adsorption isotherm data can be described by the physisorption-based isotherm model. The D-R model considers that the adsorption process occurs as multiple-layer adsorption and involves van der Waals forces. The D-R model can be expressed as follows (Eq.(9)):
$$
\ln q_{e}=\ln q_{d}-\beta\varepsilon^{2}
$$
where $\varepsilon$ is Polanyi potential which can be calculated from $\varepsilon=R T$ ln $\left(1{+}1/C_{e}\right)$ and $q_{d}$ represents the theoretical isotherm saturation capacity. $\beta$ denotes the D-R isotherm constant. Plots of ln $q_{e}$ versus $\varepsilon^{2}$ can be found in Fig. S5(d). The data points at various temperatures could not be well fitted by the linear regression using the D-R isotherm, suggesting that the fluoride adsorption to $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was not mainly attributed to physisorption.
3.6 Activation energy and thermodynamics of the adsorption of fluoride to UiO-66-NH2
To determine the activation energy of adsorption, the Arrhenius equation was adopted using the pseudo second order rate constants, $k_{2}$ , at different temperatures. The Arrhenius equation is expressed as follows:
$$
k_{2}=k_{0}\exp(-\frac{E_{a}}{R T})
$$
$$
\ln k_{2}=\ln k_{0}-{\frac{E_{a}}{R}}{\frac{1}{T}}
$$
A plot of ln $k_{2}$ versus $1/\mathrm{T}$ is shown in Fig. S6(a) (see $\mathbf{ESI\dagger}$ ), in which the data points are properly fitted by the linear regression, showing that the relationship between $k_{2}$ and temperature can be described by the Arrhenius equation. The activation energy, $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ and the temperature-independent factor, $k_{0}$ , were calculated and summarized in Table 3.
On the other hand, the isotherm data at different temperatures can be also utilized to obtain the Gibbs free energy $(\Delta\mathbf{G}^{\circ})$ , enthalpy $(\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ})$ as well as entropy $(\Delta\mathbf{S}^{\circ})$ of the adsorption using the following equations (Eqs.(12) and (13)):
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{\Delta G^{\circ}=-R T\ln K_{L}}}\\ {{\Delta G^{\circ}=\Delta H^{\circ}-T\Delta S^{\circ}}}\end{array}
$$
where $K_{L}$ is the Langmuir adsorption constant in $\mathrm{~L~mol}^{-1}$ . Table 3 shows $\Delta\mathbf{G}^{\circ}$ of the adsorption of fluoride to $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ at different temperatures. $\Delta\mathbf{G}^{\circ}\mathrm{at}20{-}60\ {}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are all negative, showing that the adsorption of fluoride to UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ can be spontaneous within this temperature range. However, ΔG° becomes less negative when the temperature is increased, revealing that the adsorption process is less favorable at high temperatures. According to the Eq.(13), a plot of $\Delta\mathbf{G}^{\circ}$ versus $T$ is compiled and shown in Fig. S6(b), and $\Delta\mathsf{S}^{\circ}$ and $\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}$ were determined and listed in Table 3. It can be seen that $\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}$ is a negative value, validating that the adsorption of fluoride to UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was an exothermic reaction. The value of $\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}({\sim}28\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ implies that the $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ adsorption to UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ was a physical adsorption. However, the comparison between the adsorption capacities of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and UiO-66 suggests that the fluoride adsorption to UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ might primarily involve a strong interaction between $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ and the metal site as discussed in the earlier section. The relatively low value of $\Delta\mathbf{S}^{\circ}$ suggests that changes on entropy associated with the adsorption process was insignificant.
3.7  Effects of pH, co-existing ions and surfactants on adsorption capacity of UiO-66-NH2
Considering that pH of wastewater can span a wide range, the effect of $\mathrm{pH}$ was also examined. Fig. 5(a) shows a plot of adsorption capacity versus pH value. When $\mathrm{\pH=}$
7 (i.e., pH-unadjusted solutions), the equilibrium adsorption capacity, $q_{e}$ , was 44.25 $\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ . When pH changed from 7 to 3, $q_{e}$ only increased slightly to $47.65~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ , indicating that the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was quite stable in acidic environments. In contrast, when $\mathrm{pH}$ changed from 7 to 11, $q_{e}$ significantly decreased to $26.46\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , revealing that the basic environments caused an adverse effect on the adsorption capacity of Ui ${\scriptstyle\mathrm{)}}{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{-}}66{\scriptstyle\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ . This could be attributed to the adsorption competition between $\mathrm{F}$ and $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ , thereby reducing the adsorption capacity [31, 32]. In addition, a few studies have revealed that MOFs appear to be less stable under alkaline conditions [54]. Thus, the XRD pattern of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ after the batch-type adsorption experiment at $\mathrm{pH}=11$ was measured and shown in Fig. S7. It can be seen that the XRD pattern of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ did not change significantly after the batch-type adsorption experiment at $\mathrm{pH}=11$ , suggesting that its crystalline structure could still maintain. The BET surface areas before and after the batch-type adsorption at $\mathrm{pH}=11$ were found to be $905~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{~and~}722~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. While the surface area of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ after the batch-type adsorption became lower, the decrease (about $20\%$ ) was not remarkable. It might be attributed to the aggregation of nanocrystals and the deposition of F on the surface after the adsorption. Thus, $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ used for the F− adsorption at relatively basic condition seemed to remain quite stable. This could be owing to that the adsorption time in the present study was relatively short $(i.e.,\sim1$ hour) compared to harsh conditions (e.g., pH 1 and 14) in the reported studies for investigating the stability of MOFs [54].
Besides, it is also critical to investigate the adsorption capacity of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ for fluoride in the presence of other ions, especially halogen ions such as chloride and bromide. Therefore, the effect of co-existing halogen ions was also examined by adding equal-concentration of chloride or bromide to fluoride-containing solutions.
Fig. 5(b) shows that UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ removed fluoride in the presence of chloride as much as it could remove in the absence of chloride. Even though $C_{0}$ changed from 10 to $70\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ , the effect of chloride on the removal amount of fluoride by UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ still remained almost unchanged. This result also agrees with the findings of the effect of $\mathrm{pH}<7$ on the adsorption capacity, in which $\mathsf{p H}$ of solutions were lowered using HCl solutions. The addition of HCl released $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ into the solutions. Since the competition adsorption between $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{F}$ was insignificant, the adsorption capacities of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ in the acidic solutions remained similar to that in the neutral solution.
When the co-existing ion became $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ , we also observed the similar result, in which UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ could remove the same amount of fluoride regardless whether $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ was present in the fluoride solution or not. These results unveil that UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ possesses a high selectivity towards fluoride, enabling UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ to be a promising and selective adsorbent to remove fluoride in wastewater. Furthermore, the removal of $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ from tap water instead of the D.I. water was also evaluated as shown in Fig. S8 (see ESI†). It can be seen that the adsorption kinetics of $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ to $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in the tap water seems almost comparable to that in the D.I. water, suggesting that the adsorption of F− to UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was not interfered significantly by ions in the tap water. We also investigated whether major anions (i.e., $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ and ${\mathrm{SO}_{4}}^{2-}$ ) in the tap water were adsorbed to UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ . As revealed in Fig. S8, almost no $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ and ${\mathrm{SO}_{4}}^{2-}$ were removed by UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ . This is consistent with the findings obtained in the effect of co-existing ions on the $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ adsorption.
In addition to the effects of $\mathrm{pH}$ and as the co-existing halogen ions, the effect of the ionic surfactants is also required to be examined considering their highly-concentrated existence in wastewater [55, 56]. These ionic surfactants might interact with fluoride or $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and therefore affect the adsorption capacity. In this study, CTAB was selected to represent a common cationic surfactant in wastewater, whereas SDS was chosen as a common anionic surfactant [55, 56]. Fig. 5(c) reveals the adsorption capacity of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ for fluoride in the presence of the surfactants. As the concentrations of the surfactants increased, the adsorption capacity of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ gradually changed. In the case of CTAB, the adsorption capacity of UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ slightly increased when $C_{0}$ of CTAB varied from 0 to $2000\ \mathrm{mg{L}^{-1}}$ . This suggests that the presence of CTAB caused a positive effect on the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . This could be attributed to the accumulation of cationic CTAB molecules on the surface of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ which in turn attracted some fluoride ions via the positive charge of CTAB. On the other hand, the adsorption capacity was found to decrease when SDS, an anionic surfactant, was added to the solutions. The adsorption capacity became much lower as the concentration of SDS in the solution was higher. This phenomenon could be ascribed to the electrostatic repulsion from the deposition of SDS on the surface of UiO-66-NH2.
Considering the findings above, it can be deduced that $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ can exhibit relatively stable capacity for removal of fluoride under acidic conditions and in the presence of other halogen ions. However, the adsorption capacity of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ for fluoride can be significantly affected under basic conditions and in the presence of ionic molecules, such as surfactants.
## 3.8 The column test using UiO-66-NH2 for the removal of $\boldsymbol{F}^{-}$
Although UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ exhibits a promising adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ in terms of batch-type experimental evaluations, it is more likely to employ $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ as a column of packed bed to remove $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ from water. Herein, a micro-column packed-bed of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was prepared and a breakthrough curve for the $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ adsorption to
UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ can be seen in Fig. S9 (see $\mathbf{ESI\dagger}$ ). The packed bed of UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (0.3 g) could continuously treat $400~\mathrm{ml}$ of $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ solution with $20~\mathrm{mg~L}^{-1}$ and the adsorption capacity of the packed bed was around $40\ \mathrm{mg\g^{-1}}$ , demonstrating the feasibility of using UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ for a continuous treatment of $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ in water.
## 4 Conclusion
In the present study, we have synthesized UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ , a zirconium-based MOF with a primary amine group on its ligand. Its crystalline structure remained intact after being exposed to water and fluoride. The local structure of zirconium in UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ did not change after being exposed to water and fluoride under the various conditions according to XANES and EXAFS results. The adsorption capacity was lower at a higher temperature, revealing the exothermic characteristic of the fluoride adsorption to $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . The enthalpy of the adsorption indicates that the $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ adsorption was classified as a physical adsorption. However, the comparison between the adsorption capacities of UiO-66-NH2 and UiO-66 suggests that the fluoride adsorption to UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ might primarily involve a strong interaction between $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ and the metal site. UiO-66-NH2 can also exhibit relatively stable capacity for removal of fluoride under acidic conditions and in the presence of other halogen ions. The stability, kinetics, adsorption isotherm and thermodynamics of fluoride adsorption to UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ were investigated in this work. These important results can provide insightful information to further optimize the adsorption process for removal of fluoride using zirconium-based MOFs.
